<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luang Prabang</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Luang Phrabang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Luang Prabang is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>, the early royal capital of Lan Xang and later the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-luang-phrabang">Kingdom of Luang Phrabang</a>, formed when Lan Xang split in 1707. Under French rule from 1893 its kings kept their thrones as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>. In 1946 the Lao lands were united under the king of Luang Phrabang as the Kingdom of Laos.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luang-prabang.jpg" alt="Luang Prabang" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 松岡明芳 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phou_si_Luang_Prabang_Laos_%E3%83%97%E3%83%BC%E3%82%B7%E3%83%BC%E3%81%AE%E4%B8%98_%E3%83%A9%E3%82%AA%E3%82%B9%E3%83%BB%E3%83%AB%E3%82%A2%E3%83%B3%E3%83%97%E3%83%A9%E3%83%90%E3%83%BC%E3%83%B3_DSCF6777.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Luang Prabang is the capital of Luang Prabang Province in north-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>. Its name means Royal Buddha Image and comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phra-bang">Phra Bang</a>, a statue that symbolises Lao sovereignty. It was historically known as Xieng Thong. UNESCO designated the city a World Heritage Site in 1995 for its blend of Lao architecture, European colonial buildings and more than thirty Buddhist temples, and its protected area covers 33 of its 58 villages. Daily rituals such as the morning procession of monks accepting alms continue there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By the 8th century the Mon had pushed north from the Chao Phraya valley and created city states, among them Muang Sua, the site of Luang Prabang. These states introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a>, brought by missionaries from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. The city experienced a period of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> suzerainty under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a> from 1185 to 1191. In 1353 it became the capital of the kingdom of Lan Xang under its first king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fa-ngum">Fa Ngum</a>. In 1359 the Khmer king at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phra-bang">Phra Bang</a> to his son-in-law Fa Ngum to give Buddhist legitimacy to his rule, and the capital took its name from the image. Vietnamese forces occupied it during Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-thanh-tong">Lê Thánh Tông</a>'s expedition of 1478 to 1480 against Lan Xang and Lanna.</p><p>In 1707 Lan Xang fell apart in a dynastic struggle, and Luang Prabang became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-luang-phrabang">Kingdom of Luang Phrabang</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>, the French recognised it as the royal residence, and its ruler gradually came to be regarded as the figurehead of Laos. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> it was held in turn by Vichy France, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and Free France. On 8 April 1945 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisavang-vong">Sisavang Vong</a> declared an end to Laos' status as a French protectorate, but after Japan's surrender he assured returning Free French forces that Laos remained one.</p><p>In April and May 1946 the French used paratroops to retake Luang Prabang and Vientiane and drove the Lao Issara ministers out of the country. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-indochina-war">First Indochina War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pathet-lao">Pathet Lao</a> forces tried to capture the city in 1953 and 1954, but French forces stopped them before they reached it. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> became independent, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisavang-vong">Sisavang Vong</a>, king of Luang Prabang, became head of state of the Kingdom of Laos.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Luang Prabang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cambodia and Laos remained monarchies under the kings at Phnom Penh and <b>Luang Phrabang</b>, supervised by French residents, while much of Laos was administered directly.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Setthathirath moved the capital to Vientiane in 1560, building the Haw Phra Kaew to house the Emerald Buddha and the great stupa of That Luang, while the old capital received the name <b>Luang Prabang</b> after the Phra Bang.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-xang">Lan Xang · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1479 his armies invaded the Lao kingdom of Lan Xang and briefly occupied its capital, <b>Luang Prabang</b>, before withdrawing with heavy losses.</blockquote><a href="/empire/later-le-dynasty">Later Le dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>France ruled the kingdom of <b>Luang Phrabang</b> as a protectorate and the rest of Laos directly, through provinces with French officials and Vietnamese clerks.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-protectorate-of-laos">French protectorate of Laos</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1946 France and King Sisavang Vong agreed to unite <b>Luang Phrabang</b>, Vientiane, Champasak and other Lao provinces into a single kingdom, with Prince Boun Oum of Champasak renouncing his claims.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-laos">Kingdom of Laos</a></li><li><blockquote>During the succession conflicts after Sourigna Vongsa's death, his grandson Kingkitsarath, with backing from Sipsongpanna, established himself at <b>Luang Prabang</b> in 1707 as king, separate from the court of the Kingdom of Vientiane.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-luang-phrabang">Kingdom of Luang Phrabang</a></li></ul></section>
